From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:09:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 81/81] web-app: let settings record a test clip and play it
 back
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Record without noise filtering" is a switch whose effect you can only
judge by ear, and its description has to hedge ("some browsers and
microphones ignore this setting") because the constraints are advisory.
Both problems have the same answer: record a few seconds and listen.

Adds a Test the microphone row under the switch. It records a 5s take
with the setting as it currently stands, plays it back through the
shared CustomAudioControls, and — when recording unfiltered was asked
for — says whether the browser actually honored it, read back from
track.getSettings() via the already-tested originalAudioHonored(). That
readback lost its only surface when the recorder pill went away; this
gives it a better one, next to the switch it describes and available
without starting a real voice message.

Not live monitoring: routing the mic to the speaker is what causes the
feedback howl, and the mitigation apps rely on for that is echo
cancellation — exactly what this setting disables. So live playback
would be safe only while the setting is off, i.e. never when you want to
test it. Record-then-play answers the same question with no feedback
path.

Refs #179
